  "summary": "# Daily Health-Tech Digest – July 30, 2026\n\nThe regulatory and competitive landscape continues shifting across multiple sectors. The FTC sued Hims & Hers for allegedly misleading consumers and violating privacy, while the FDA expert panel recommended against approving Capricor's Duchenne muscular dystrophy drug. On the drug development front, competitors are emerging in high-value markets—Latigo reported mid-stage success for an acute-pain drug aimed at rivaling Vertex's Journavx, and Altimmune showed phase-two efficacy for a GLP-1 drug reducing heavy drinking in alcohol use disorder trials.\n\nHealthcare IT remains dominated by Epic, though a survey of 112 health system executives shows startups can still win by solving specific problems or offering superior ROI. Key tech themes include the growing deployment of AI in clinical workflows and patient monitoring, the emergence of genomic foundation models for personalized care, and ongoing struggles with benchmarking clinical language models for safety and accuracy. Interoperability frameworks are advancing through CMS initiatives, but implementation challenges persist around cloud maturity and data fragmentation.\n\nWorkforce and policy issues underscore broader strategic shifts. The NSF is piloting a new Ph.D. model blending academic and industry experience to stem young scientists' exodus from academia. Meanwhile, ending a Medicare drug subsidy has become a political attack line heading into the midterms, and prior authorization automation is becoming a strategic imperative as staffing constraints threaten revenue and access. Psychiatry's digital transformation and trauma-informed care gaps in mainstream medicine also appeared as emerging priorities across coverage.",
